#{[17592186323752 :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,1,0)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-1,1,0)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(1)(0)+(0)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}-(-1)(1)+(-1)(0)-(1)(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+0+0}{1+1+0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186330301 :answer/value "-7tan⁡x+4csc⁡x+C"] [17592186330616 :answer/value "-7tan⁡x-4csc⁡x+C"] [17592186330842 :answer/value "-3csc⁡x-2cot⁡x+C"] [17592186330929 :answer/value "7tan⁡x+4sec⁡x+C"] [17592186331183 :answer/value "-2cos⁡x+2tan⁡x+C"] [17592186331413 :answer/value "3csc⁡x-2sec⁡x+C"] [17592186331704 :answer/value "-3csc⁡x-2tan⁡x+C"] [17592186331724 :answer/value "7cot⁡x+4sec⁡x+C"] [17592186331771 :answer/value "3{sin}^{3}⁡x-2cot⁡x+C"] [17592186332047 :answer/value "7cot⁡x-4sec⁡x+C"] [17592186335688 :question/worked-solution "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=4x+3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=6xy-{x}^{3}.$\n\nLet's check if the equation is exact. To do this, we check to see if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y)=6y-3{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy-{x}^{3})=6y-3{x}^{2}=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, the given equation is exact and its solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (4x+3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}y+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (6xy-{x}^{3})\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}y+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=2{x}^{2},\\,f(y)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}y=c.\n$$"] [17592186336183 :answer/value "cos⁡x+5cot⁡x+C"] [17592186336295 :answer/value "-cos⁡x+5cot⁡x+C"] [17592186336421 :answer/value "sec⁡ytan⁡y+C"] [17592186336517 :answer/value "4sec⁡\\theta +C"] [17592186336591 :answer/value "3tan⁡y+C"] [17592186336617 :answer/value "5csc⁡\\theta +C"] [17592186336736 :answer/value "tan⁡y+C"] [17592186336781 :answer/value "-cos⁡x-5cot⁡x+C"] [17592186336801 :answer/value "-csc+5tan⁡x+C"] [17592186336834 :answer/value "-5sec⁡\\theta +C"] [17592186336853 :answer/value "-tan⁡y+C"] [17592186336905 :answer/value "csc-5tan⁡x+C"] [17592186336913 :answer/value "5sec⁡\\theta +C"]}
